Great habit — understand before you execute. Let me break down all 3 files.

---

**The big picture first**

Think of Terraform like ordering from a restaurant. `variables.tf` is the menu (what options exist), `main.tf` is your order (what you actually want), and `outputs.tf` is the receipt (what you get back after your order is fulfilled).

---

**File 1 — `variables.tf` (the menu)**

This file just defines configurable values so you never hardcode things in `main.tf`. For example instead of writing `us-east-1` everywhere, you define it once here. If you ever want to deploy to a different region, you change it in one place. This is exactly how PIMCO would manage dev vs staging vs prod — same `main.tf`, different variable values.

---

**File 2 — `main.tf` (your order — the most important file)**

This is where you actually define the 3 AWS resources Terraform will create:

**Resource 1 — Key Pair.** This is how you SSH into your EC2 server. You give AWS your public SSH key, AWS stores it, and when EC2 starts it puts that key on the server. Only someone with the matching private key (you) can log in. This is how every engineer connects to a production server securely — no passwords.

**Resource 2 — Security Group.** This is the firewall around your EC2 instance. It controls what traffic is allowed in and out. We open port 22 for SSH (so you can connect) and port 8080 for your app (so the world can call your API). Everything else is blocked. At Citi this is what kept their payment servers secure.

**Resource 3 — EC2 Instance.** This is the actual Linux server in AWS. `t2.micro` is free tier — costs you nothing. Inside it there's a `user_data` script — this is a bash script that runs automatically the very first time EC2 boots up. It installs Docker on the server so it's ready to run your container the moment GitHub Actions deploys to it.

---

**File 3 — `outputs.tf` (the receipt)**

After Terraform creates everything, you need to know the IP address of your server to connect to it and to give GitHub Actions. `outputs.tf` tells Terraform to print those values when it finishes. Without this you'd have to go dig through the AWS console to find them.

---

**How all 3 work together — the exact sequence:**

```
You run: terraform apply
    ↓
Terraform reads variables.tf  →  gets region, ami, key path
    ↓
Terraform reads main.tf       →  creates key pair, security group, EC2
    ↓
EC2 boots → user_data runs → Docker gets installed automatically
    ↓
Terraform reads outputs.tf    →  prints your EC2 IP and DNS
    ↓
You take that IP → give it to GitHub Actions → pipeline deploys your app
```

